  <div id="lessonContent">
    <h2>1. What is a Loop?</h2>
    <p><b>Loop = Repeat the same work again and again, automatically.</b></p>
    <p>Example: You want to print 1 to 100. Without a loop, you must write 100 lines. With a loop, you write only 3 lines, and JavaScript repeats it 100 times for you.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>Real Life Example:</b> A washing machine repeats the same cycle - Wash, Rinse, Spin. A fan keeps rotating. A loop is the same idea - Do the same work until the condition becomes false.</div>
    <h2>2. LIVE DEMO - Multiplication Table Generator</h2>
    <p>Type any number. Loop will create its full table:</p>
    <div class="demo-card">
      <h3 id="demoTableTitle" style="margin:0">Table of 5</h3>
      <p id="demoTable" style="color:#222;margin:10px 0;line-height:1.9;font-weight:bold">Press Generate to see table</p>
      <input class="demo-input" id="tableInput" type="number" placeholder="Enter number - e.g. 7" value="5">
      <button class="btn btn-js" onclick="makeTable()">Generate Table ✖️</button>
      <button class="btn btn-next" onclick="printNumbers()">Print 1 to 10 🔢</button>
      <button class="btn" onclick="resetDemo()">Reset ↺</button>
    </div>
    <h2>3. for Loop - Most Used Loop</h2>
    <p><b>for loop</b> is used when you know how many times to repeat. Example: Repeat 10 times, repeat 100 times.</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">console.log("Hello " + i);</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span><br><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Output:</span><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Hello 1</span><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Hello 2</span><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Hello 3</span><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Hello 4</span><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Hello 5</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>4. Understand for Loop - 3 Parts</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Part</th><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Runs How Many Times?</th></tr>
      <tr><td>1. Start</td><td>let i = 1;</td><td>Start from 1</td><td>Only 1 time - At start</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2. Condition</td><td>i &lt;= 5;</td><td>Run until i is 5 or less</td><td>Every time - Before each repeat</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3. Update</td><td>i++</td><td>Increase i by 1 after each repeat</td><td>Every time - After each repeat</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p><b>i++</b> means <code>i = i + 1</code>. It is a shortcut. <code>i--</code> means <code>i = i - 1</code>.</p>
    <h2>5. while Loop</h2>
    <p><b>while loop</b> is used when you do not know how many times to repeat. It runs until the condition becomes false.</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">let i = 1;</span><br><br>
<span class="text">while (i <= 5) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">console.log("Number " + i);</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">i++; // Very Important - Otherwise infinite loop!</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span>
    </code></div>
    <div class="note"><b>Warning - Infinite Loop:</b> If you forget <code>i++</code> in a while loop, the loop will never stop. Your browser will hang. Always increase the counter inside a while loop.</div>
    <h2>6. do-while Loop</h2>
    <p><b>do-while</b> runs at least 1 time, even if the condition is false. Because it checks the condition after running.</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">let i = 10;</span><br><br>
<span class="text">do {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">console.log("Hello " + i);</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">i++;</span><br>
<span class="text">} while (i <= 5);</span><br><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Output: Hello 10 - Runs 1 time even though 10 is not <= 5</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>7. break and continue</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>break</b></td><td>Stop the loop completely</td><td>If i == 5, break - Loop stops at 5</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>continue</b></td><td>Skip this one, go to next</td><td>If i == 5, continue - Skip 5, print others</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// break Example - Print 1 to 10, but stop at 5</span><br>
<span class="text">for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">if (i == 6) { break; }</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">console.log(i); // 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span><br><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// continue Example - Print 1 to 5, but skip 3</span><br>
<span class="text">for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">if (i == 3) { continue; }</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">console.log(i); // 1, 2, 4, 5 - 3 is skipped</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>8. Complete Example - Multiplication Table</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;body&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;h1</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"table"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Table</span><span class="tag">&lt;/h1&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;input</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"num"</span> <span class="attr">type</span>=<span class="val">"number"</span> <span class="attr">placeholder</span>=<span class="val">"Enter Number"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;button</span> <span class="attr">onclick</span>=<span class="val">"makeTable()"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Make Table</span><span class="tag">&lt;/button&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">function makeTable() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let n = Number(document.getElementById("num").value);</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let result = "";</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">result = result + n + " x " + i + " = " + (n * i) + "&lt;br&gt;";</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">}</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">document.getElementById("table").innerHTML = result;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">}</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/body&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/html&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>9. Homework</h2>
    <p>In Playground, build this:</p>
    <p>1. Use for loop to print 1 to 100 in console<br>2. Use for loop to print Even numbers 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 - Hint: Use <code>i = i + 2</code> or check <code>i % 2 == 0</code><br>3. Make a Multiplication Table generator like the demo above - input number, loop 1 to 10, show table</p>
    <div class="box"><b>Remember:</b> Loop = Repeat work | for = When you know how many times | while = When you do not know how many times | do-while = Runs at least 1 time | i++ = Increase by 1 | break = Stop loop | continue = Skip this one | Never forget i++ in while loop</div>
    <p><b>Next Step:</b> <a href="/lesson26">Lesson 26 - Functions</a> - Learn how to make reusable code blocks. Write once, use many times.</p>
